// A document database, as the vocabulary's `database` kind at `type: document`.
//
// # A document crosses as JSON
//
// The canonical ABI has no arbitrary map, and a document IS one — nested,
// heterogeneous, whatever the author put in it. Declaring a variant deep enough
// to carry that would be declaring a JSON type in WIT, which every guest would
// then translate to and from its own JSON anyway. So the bytes ARE the JSON,
// and a guest uses the parser it already has.
//
// THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT: it is what makes this interface stable while the
// shape of a document is the author's business. The one thing lost is that the
// host cannot check the shape, and it does not need to — it stores what it is
// given and answers with what it stored.
//
// # It is shaped like the client the author already uses
//
// get/put/delete by id, plus a find over a filter document. That is MongoDB's
// and Firestore's and CouchDB's shape with the engine-specific half left out:
// the filter is equality on the fields it names, which every one of them
// supports, and none of `$gt`, aggregation pipelines or map-reduce is here
// because they do not survive the trip between engines.
//
// # `find` has no page, and that is a known ceiling
//
// It answers the whole match set as one value, so a filter matching a large
// collection materialises all of it in the guest's memory. A page needs a
// SORT to be meaningful — a cursor over an unordered result answers differently
// on every call — and the four engines this could sit on do not agree on how a
// sort is spelled, which is the same reason `$gt` is not here. Until they do,
// a caller that needs bounded reads narrows the filter.
package taubyte:document@0.1.0;

interface store {
    variant error {
        no-such-database(string),
        denied(string),
        not-found,
        // The bytes were not a JSON object, in or out.
        malformed(string),
        unavailable(string),
    }

    get: func(name: string, id: string) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    put: func(name: string, id: string, doc: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;
    delete: func(name: string, id: string) -> result<_, error>;
    // The filter is a JSON object: equality on every field it names, with a
    // dotted name descending into a nested one. Empty means everything.
    //
    // IT IS A string AND THE ANSWER IS BYTES, which is not an inconsistency: a
    // guest builds a filter as text and would have to copy it into a byte list
    // for nothing, while a result set is bytes it will hand straight to a
    // parser.
    find: func(name: string, filter: string) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
}

world imports {
    import store;
}
